---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import SemesterTrack from "../components/SemesterTrack.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.svg";
import "../styles/site.css";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="Five bars of a rating curve, from below expectations to exceeds, with the tall middle bar labelled you"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Learn how large technology companies decide who exceeds, who meets, and who
    is on the list. Then learn the tricks people use to move themselves up
    that list, one a week, and use each one on your own work. Then find out
    why each trick works, from the real case it comes from, with the sources
    to check. In week 10 the tricks stop working, and in week 12 you sit on
    the other side of the table and design a review your week-1 self could
    not beat. You leave with that design, a brag document nine weeks long, and the
    ability to read any rubric as its author.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Final-year students about to start a graduate job at a company with a
    performance review. If you have ever been rated, ranked or calibrated and
    were not in the room when it happened, this course is about that room. No
    prerequisites. It helps to have been on a team of more than three people.
  </p>

  <h2>How the semester runs</h2>
  <SemesterTrack />
  <p>
    Lectures are on Tuesdays and give you the mechanism. Stand-ups are on
    Thursdays and give you the trick. Each week's trick has a source; no
    trick in the course is invented.
  </p>

  <h2>A note on marks</h2>
  <p>
    Marks in this course are calibrated to a curve you will not be shown. The
    <a href={withBase("/policies/")}>policies page</a> says so in full. It is the only
    honest way to run this particular course.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>Why each trick works, the real case behind it, and the sources.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The trick of the week, what to bring, and what happens in the room.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Four pieces of work, the last of which turns the course around.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>One person who has chaired the meeting, one who is currently in it.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
